Rem ==================================================================
Rem neural.shoddy — Shoddy standard library: feed-forward neural nets
Rem
Rem One hidden layer, Tanh activation, identity output, trained by
Rem mini-batch SGD on squared-error loss — the classic regression
Rem network. A Net is a record of two weight matrices and two bias
Rem vectors; a GRADIENT HAS THE SAME SHAPE AS THE NETWORK, so the same
Rem record represents both and accumulate / average / apply-update all
Rem fall out of NetAdd and NetScale — no zeroing loops, no mutable
Rem scratch arrays.
Rem
Rem Scale: like stats.shoddy this is classroom-sized — hundreds of rows
Rem and a few thousand epochs through immutable arrays, not bulk
Rem training.
Rem
Rem Impure edges (the only two, both via random.shoddy): NetNew draws
Rem its initial weights from Rnd, and NetTrain shuffles each epoch. A
Rem caller wanting reproducible runs calls the Seed builtin once, at
Rem the top of Main — the same edge random.shoddy documents.
Rem
Rem Serialization, two formats over one canonical weight order (input-
Rem hidden weights input-major, hidden biases, hidden-output weights
Rem hidden-major, output biases):
Rem   * NetSave/NetLoad — text, one number per line, so weight files
Rem     interchange with the C# reference program. Str writes 10
Rem     significant digits, so a round trip reproduces predictions to
Rem     ~1e-10 relative, not to the last bit.
Rem   * NetSaveBin/NetLoadBin — binary over recio, a self-describing
Rem     header (magic, ni, nh, no) then raw 8-byte IEEE doubles, so a
Rem     round trip is BIT-EXACT and loading needs no architecture
Rem     arguments.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "matrix.shoddy"
Include "random.shoddy"
Include "file.shoddy"
Include "recio.shoddy"

Rem W1 rows are hidden neurons: MatVec(W1, x) is the hidden pre-
Rem activation in one word. NOTE this is the TRANSPOSE of the C#
Rem reference's ihWeights (input-major) layout; NetMatSerial bridges.
Type Net
    W1 As Matrix              Rem nh x ni
    B1 As Array Of Number     Rem nh
    W2 As Matrix              Rem no x nh
    B2 As Array Of Number     Rem no (1 for regression)

Rem A forward pass keeps its post-tanh hidden activations — backprop
Rem needs them, and NetOut throws them away for free.
Type NetEval
    Hidden As Array Of Number
    Output As Array Of Number

Rem ---- the gradient algebra: same shape, two words -------------------

Def NetAdd(a As Net, b As Net) As Net
    Net(MatAdd(W1(a), W1(b)), VAdd(B1(a), B1(b)), MatAdd(W2(a), W2(b)), VAdd(B2(a), B2(b)))

Def NetScale(n As Net, s As Number) As Net
    Net(MatScale(W1(n), s), VScale(B1(n), s), MatScale(W2(n), s), VScale(B2(n), s))

Rem ---- construction (IMPURE: Rnd; Seed first for reproducibility) ----

Def NetRandArray(n As Number) As Array Of Number
    ToArray(Map(Range(1, n), Fn(k) => RandomRange(-0.01, 0.01)))

Def NetNew(ni As Number, nh As Number, no As Number) As Net
    Net(Matrix(nh, ni, NetRandArray(nh * ni)), NetRandArray(nh), Matrix(no, nh, NetRandArray(no * nh)), NetRandArray(no))

Rem ---- forward -------------------------------------------------------

Def NetEvalAt(n As Net, x As Array Of Number) As NetEval
    Let h = Map(VAdd(MatVec(W1(n), x), B1(n)), Tanh)
    NetEval(h, VAdd(MatVec(W2(n), h), B2(n)))

Def NetOut(n As Net, x As Array Of Number) As Number
    First(Output(NetEvalAt(n, x)))

Rem ---- backward ------------------------------------------------------

Rem One example's gradient of squared error (no 1/2 factor), identity
Rem output. oSig = o - y; hSig = W2^T oSig  ⊙  (1 - h^2), with the tanh
Rem derivative written (1-h)(1+h); the weight gradients are outer
Rem products of a signal with the layer's input.
Def NetGrad(n As Net, x As Array Of Number, y As Number) As Net
    Let e = NetEvalAt(n, x)
    Let h = Hidden(e)
    Let oSig = Map(Output(e), Fn(v) => v - y)
    Let hSig = VMul(MatVec(Transp(W2(n)), oSig), Map(h, Fn(v) => (1 - v) * (1 + v)))
    Net(Outer(hSig, x), hSig, Outer(oSig, h), oSig)

Rem One mini-batch step: average the per-example gradients, descend.
Def NetStep(n As Net, xs As List Of Array Of Number, ys As List Of Number, lr As Number) As Net
    Let grads = ZipWith(xs, ys, Fn(x, y) => NetGrad(n, x, y))
    Let avg = NetScale(Fold(Rest(grads), First(grads), NetAdd), 1 / Length(grads))
    NetAdd(n, NetScale(avg, Negate(lr)))

Rem ---- training ------------------------------------------------------

Def NetBatch(net As Net, xa As Array Of Array Of Number, ya As Array Of Number, perm As Array Of Number, b As Number, batSize As Number, lr As Number) As Net
    Let idx = Map(Range((b - 1) * batSize + 1, b * batSize), Fn(k) => Nth(perm, k))
    NetStep(net, Map(idx, Fn(i) => Nth(xa, i)), Map(idx, Fn(i) => Nth(ya, i)), lr)

Rem One epoch: shuffle the row indices (IMPURE: Rnd), then tile the
Rem whole permutation with Floor(n / batSize) consecutive chunks —
Rem the remainder rows are dropped, matching the C# int division.
Def NetEpoch(net As Net, xa As Array Of Array Of Number, ya As Array Of Number, lr As Number, batSize As Number) As Net
    Let perm = ToArray(Shuffle(Range(1, Length(ya))))
    Fold(Range(1, Floor(Length(ya) / batSize)), net, Fn(nn, b) => NetBatch(nn, xa, ya, perm, b, batSize, lr))

Def NetReport(net As Net, epoch As Number, report As [ Number Net -- ]) As Net
    Call(report, epoch, net)
    net

Rem Fold over epochs. report is called with (epoch, net) after EVERY
Rem epoch — the caller decides when to print and pay for metrics, so
Rem both the effect and the cost stay at the mill's edge.
Def NetTrain(n As Net, xs As List Of Array Of Number, ys As List Of Number, lr As Number, batSize As Number, epochs As Number, report As [ Number Net -- ]) As Net
    Let xa = ToArray(xs)
    Let ya = ToArray(ys)
    Fold(Range(1, epochs), n, Fn(net, epoch) => NetReport(NetEpoch(net, xa, ya, lr, batSize), epoch, report))

Rem ---- metrics -------------------------------------------------------

Def NetSqErr(n As Net, x As Array Of Number, y As Number) As Number
    Let d = NetOut(n, x) - y
    d * d

Def NetMse(n As Net, xs As List Of Array Of Number, ys As List Of Number) As Number
    Fold(ZipWith(xs, ys, Fn(x, y) => NetSqErr(n, x, y)), 0, +) / Length(ys)

Rem The fraction of rows predicted within pctClose of the actual value
Rem (|pred - actual| < pctClose * |actual|) — percent-close accuracy.
Def NetClose(n As Net, x As Array Of Number, y As Number, pctClose As Number) As Number
    If Abs(NetOut(n, x) - y) < Abs(pctClose * y) Then
        1
    Else
        0

Def NetAccuracy(n As Net, xs As List Of Array Of Number, ys As List Of Number, pctClose As Number) As Number
    Fold(ZipWith(xs, ys, Fn(x, y) => NetClose(n, x, y, pctClose)), 0, +) / Length(ys)

Rem ---- save / load (effectful, like all file words) ------------------

Rem The C# demo serializes ih weights INPUT-major (for i in inputs, for
Rem j in hidden) — that is column-major over our W1, whose layout is
Rem the transpose of the C# ihWeights. Transp-then-flat-cells walks
Rem exactly that order (and hidden-major over W2, likewise). This is
Rem where a silent transpose bug would live; the finite-difference and
Rem round-trip tests in tst/neuraltest.shoddy guard it.
Def NetMatSerial(m As Matrix) As List Of Number
    ToList(Cells(Transp(m)))

Def NetWeightList(n As Net) As List Of Number
    Concat(Concat(NetMatSerial(W1(n)), ToList(B1(n))), Concat(NetMatSerial(W2(n)), ToList(B2(n))))

Def NetSave(path As String, n As Net)
    WriteLines(path, Map(NetWeightList(n), Str))

Def NetSlice(ws As Array Of Number, start As Number, count As Number) As Array Of Number
    ToArray(Map(Range(start, start + count - 1), Fn(k) => Nth(ws, k)))

Rem The inverse of NetWeightList: a Net from a flat weight array in the
Rem serialization order above.
Def NetFromList(ws As Array Of Number, ni As Number, nh As Number, no As Number) As Net
    Let w1 = Transp(Matrix(ni, nh, NetSlice(ws, 1, ni * nh)))
    Let b1 = NetSlice(ws, ni * nh + 1, nh)
    Let w2 = Transp(Matrix(nh, no, NetSlice(ws, ni * nh + nh + 1, nh * no)))
    Let b2 = NetSlice(ws, ni * nh + nh + nh * no + 1, no)
    Net(w1, b1, w2, b2)

Def NetLoad(path As String, ni As Number, nh As Number, no As Number) As Net
    Let ws = ToArray(Map(ReadLines(path), Val))
    If Length(ws) <> ni * nh + nh + nh * no + no Then
        Error("NETLOAD: WRONG NUMBER OF WEIGHTS FOR " & Str(ni) & "-" & Str(nh) & "-" & Str(no))
    Else
        NetFromList(ws, ni, nh, no)

Rem ---- binary save / load (over recio; bit-exact) --------------------

Rem A model file is a stream of 8-byte doubles: a 4-double header —
Rem the magic (1986, backpropagation's year), then ni, nh, no — then
Rem the weights in the canonical order above. Every double is read and
Rem written through recio's PutRec/GetRec at an explicit 1-based slot,
Rem so nothing leans on sequential file-position state.

Def NetBinMagic() As Number
    1986

Def NetPutAt(f As Number, k As Number, v As Number)
    PutRec(f, k, 8, PutNum, v)

Def NetGetAt(f As Number, k As Number) As Number
    GetRec(f, k, 8, GetNum)

Def NetSaveBin(path As String, n As Net)
    If FileExists(path) Then
        DeleteFile(path)
    Let f = BOpen(path)
    NetPutAt(f, 1, NetBinMagic())
    NetPutAt(f, 2, Cols(W1(n)))
    NetPutAt(f, 3, Rows(W1(n)))
    NetPutAt(f, 4, Rows(W2(n)))
    Let ws = ToArray(NetWeightList(n))
    Each(Range(1, Length(ws)), Fn(k) => NetPutAt(f, k + 4, Nth(ws, k)))
    BClose(f)

Rem Self-describing: the header carries the architecture, so no
Rem ni/nh/no arguments. The magic and the exact byte size are checked
Rem before any weight is trusted.
Def NetLoadBin(path As String) As Net
    Let f = BOpen(path)
    If BSize(f) < 32 Or NetGetAt(f, 1) <> NetBinMagic() Then
        Error("NETLOADBIN: NOT A NEURAL MODEL FILE: " & path)
    Let ni = NetGetAt(f, 2)
    Let nh = NetGetAt(f, 3)
    Let no = NetGetAt(f, 4)
    Let count = ni * nh + nh + nh * no + no
    If BSize(f) <> 8 * (4 + count) Then
        Error("NETLOADBIN: WRONG SIZE FOR " & Str(ni) & "-" & Str(nh) & "-" & Str(no) & " MODEL: " & path)
    Let ws = ToArray(Map(Range(1, count), Fn(k) => NetGetAt(f, k + 4)))
    BClose(f)
    NetFromList(ws, ni, nh, no)
